questions designed by the ANAES. RESULTS Of the 10,532 subjects interviewed, 1,179 subjects (21.3%) were identified as 
migraineurs. Sixty percent of all migraine subjects were not self-aware that they had migraine. Medical consultation, 
duration of migraine history, severe intensity of attacks, impact on daily living, and female gender promoted 
self-awareness of migraine. On the other hand, HRQoL and anxiety and depression scores were not different between 
subjects self-aware or not self-aware of migraine. Only 20% of all migraine subjects were medically followed-up. Quality
